-- allium: 3
-- rotation-server.allium

use "./rotation-daemon.allium" as daemon

-- Scope: Bitwarden server side of credential rotation
-- Includes:
--   Target system registration and management
--   Rotation configuration (linking a cipher to a target system, with schedule,
--     mode and settings)
--   Rotation scheduling and triggering: on a schedule, on demand, and on access
--     events (an upstream grant over the credential ending)
--   Manual rotation fallback for systems that cannot be rotated automatically:
--     tracking the obligation and recording an out-of-band rotation
--   Task dispatch and server-side tracking, including recovery when the daemon
--     connection drops
--   Audit emission for every rotation attempt and outcome (Goal 4)
-- Excludes:
--   Daemon execution of rotations (governed by rotation-daemon.allium)
--   The daemon authentication handshake and session lifecycle (modelled in
--     rotation-daemon.allium); this spec covers only daemon registration/revocation
--   Bitwarden cipher/Secrets Manager internals (external systems)
--   Audit-log persistence and WORM/tamper-evidence: this spec emits the audit
--     events; durable, append-only storage is the existing Phase 1 audit log's
--     concern (mirrors how pam.allium models LeaseActivationAudited)
--   Discovery of privileged/orphaned accounts, ephemeral account provisioning,
--     dependency orchestration beyond the credential, and post-rotation scripting
--     (Phase 1 non-goals)
--   Rotation of Secrets Manager secrets (Phase 1 non-goal; see the SM seam note
--     by RotationConfig and the closing open question)
-- Dependencies:
--   daemon/Cipher, daemon/TargetSystem, daemon/RotationTask, daemon/ServerConnection
--   are governed by rotation-daemon.allium and referenced here by qualified name.

config {
    failure_retry_delay: Duration = 1.hour
    -- Lifetime of the cipher-write grant (the nonce) issued with each dispatch.
    -- Must comfortably exceed one rotation attempt (target update + cipher write),
    -- since the cipher is written only after the target system has been rotated.
    cipher_write_grant_ttl: Duration = 15.minutes
}

-- Parties

-- The administrator is a Bitwarden user managing rotation. Their identity and
-- authentication are governed by the Bitwarden platform, outside this spec.
external entity Administrator {}

-- An upstream access-control system (PAM leasing, AAP grants, ...) that reports
-- when a grant over a managed credential ends. Modelled as an abstract integration
-- party so rotation depends on the access-event signal, not on any one leasing
-- spec. See AccessEventIntake and RotateOnAccessEnd.
actor AccessEventSource {
    identified_by: cipher.id
}

-- The rotation daemon, acting back on the server to write a rotated password to a
-- cipher. Its credential is scoped to the connect/task endpoints and PUT ciphers
-- only, and every cipher write must present a valid server-issued grant (the nonce).
actor RotationDaemon {
    identified_by: grant.id
}

-- Entities

entity TargetSystemRegistration {
    id: String
    target_system: daemon/TargetSystem
    status: active | disabled

    transitions status {
        active -> disabled
        disabled -> active
    }
}

-- A registered rotation daemon identity. The admin registers a daemon, the server
-- issues its credential (an OAuth2 client_id + client_secret plus a local
-- encryption key, delivered out of band; only the client_id is retained here), and
-- the daemon authenticates with it (see rotation-daemon.allium / DaemonSession).
-- Revoking a registration invalidates the credential and the daemon's session.
-- Status literals are deliberately distinct from TargetSystemRegistration's
-- (enrolled, not active) so untyped trigger-param inference stays unambiguous.
entity DaemonRegistration {
    id: String
    name: String
    client_id: String
    status: enrolled | revoked

    transitions status {
        enrolled -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

-- A rotation configuration links a cipher to a registered target system and
-- carries the rotation settings and schedule. A cipher has at most one rotation
-- configuration (which names its target system); see OneConfigPerCipher. The
-- cipher therefore uniquely identifies the configuration to rotate.
--
-- SM seam (Phase 1 non-goal): a future Secrets Manager configuration would point
-- at an SM secret rather than a cipher. The `cipher` field here and
-- daemon/RotationTask.cipher are the single seam to generalise; the scheduling,
-- dispatch, recovery and audit machinery below is intended to carry an SM rotation
-- unchanged. Kept cipher-specific for now to avoid speculative structure.
entity RotationConfig {
    id: String
    cipher: daemon/Cipher
    target_system: daemon/TargetSystem
    account_identity: String
    terminate_sessions: Boolean
    -- automatic: the daemon rotates this credential (on schedule, on demand, or on
    --   an access event). Only allowed when the target system can be rotated
    --   automatically.
    -- manual: the daemon cannot rotate this credential, so the obligation is
    --   tracked and surfaced for an admin to satisfy out of band (Goal 3).
    mode: automatic | manual
    -- Opt-in: dispatch a rotation when an access grant over this credential ends
    -- (Goal 2). Honoured only for automatic configurations.
    rotate_on_access_end: Boolean
    rotation_interval: Duration
    next_rotation_at: Timestamp
    enabled: Boolean
    last_rotation_at: Timestamp?

    dispatches: TaskDispatch with rotation_config = this
    in_flight_dispatches: dispatches where status in {dispatched, in_progress}
    has_in_flight_dispatch: in_flight_dispatches.count > 0

    -- A manual configuration that has come due: the obligation an admin must
    -- satisfy and record (see RecordManualRotation). Surfaced, never dispatched.
    awaiting_manual_rotation: mode = manual and enabled and next_rotation_at <= now
}

-- A server-side record tracking one dispatched rotation, used to observe
-- progress and to recover when the connection drops mid-execution.
entity TaskDispatch {
    id: String
    rotation_config: RotationConfig
    rotation_task: daemon/RotationTask
    status: dispatched | in_progress | completed | failed | lost
    -- Why this rotation was dispatched. Recorded as part of the rotation trail
    -- (Goal 4) and distinguishes a recovery re-dispatch from a first attempt.
    source: scheduled | on_demand | access_end | reconnect
    dispatched_at: Timestamp

    transitions status {
        dispatched -> in_progress
        dispatched -> lost
        in_progress -> completed
        in_progress -> failed
        in_progress -> lost
        terminal: completed, failed, lost
    }
}

-- The capability ("nonce") that authorises a single cipher write. Issued with each
-- dispatch, bound to the one cipher being rotated, short-lived and single-use. The
-- grant's identity is the unguessable token the daemon presents; the server accepts
-- a cipher write only against an issued, unexpired grant whose cipher matches (see
-- AcceptCipherUpdate). This scopes the daemon's write authority to exactly the
-- cipher it was dispatched, even though it holds the organization key to encrypt.
entity CipherWriteGrant {
    id: String
    dispatch: TaskDispatch
    cipher: daemon/Cipher
    created_at: Timestamp
    expires_at: Timestamp
    status: issued | consumed | expired

    transitions status {
        issued -> consumed
        issued -> expired
        terminal: consumed, expired
    }
}

-- Daemon registration

rule RegisterDaemon {
    when: RegisterDaemon(name, client_id)
    requires: not exists DaemonRegistration{client_id: client_id}
    ensures: DaemonRegistration.created(name: name, client_id: client_id, status: enrolled)
    @guidance
        -- The server issues the daemon's access token (client_id + client_secret and
        -- a local encryption key) out of band. The secret and encryption key are not
        -- retained server-side in recoverable form; only the client_id is kept, for
        -- identification (zero-knowledge, see rotation-daemon.allium).
}

rule RevokeDaemon {
    when: RevokeDaemon(registration)
    requires: registration.status = enrolled
    ensures: registration.status = revoked
    @guidance
        -- Revoking a daemon invalidates its credential. The next time the daemon
        -- interacts its session is revoked (rotation-daemon.allium /
        -- HandleSessionRevoked) and its connection closed; it cannot authenticate
        -- again until issued a new credential (see the re-issuance open question in
        -- rotation-daemon.allium).
}

-- Target system registration

rule RegisterTargetSystem {
    when: RegisterTargetSystem(target_system)
    requires: not exists TargetSystemRegistration{target_system: target_system}
    ensures: TargetSystemRegistration.created(target_system: target_system, status: active)
}

rule DisableTargetSystem {
    when: DisableTargetSystem(registration)
    requires: registration.status = active
    ensures: registration.status = disabled
    @guidance
        -- A disabled target system stops new rotations being dispatched for its
        -- configurations; in-flight dispatches are unaffected.
}

rule EnableTargetSystem {
    when: EnableTargetSystem(registration)
    requires: registration.status = disabled
    ensures: registration.status = active
}

-- Rotation configuration

rule CreateRotationConfig {
    when: CreateRotationConfig(cipher, target_system, account_identity, terminate_sessions, rotation_interval, mode, rotate_on_access_end)
    let registration = TargetSystemRegistration{target_system: target_system}
    requires: exists registration
    requires: registration.status = active
    requires: not exists RotationConfig{cipher: cipher}
    -- An automatic configuration can only target a system the daemon can rotate;
    -- a system without an automated integration must be managed manually (Goal 3).
    requires: mode = manual or target_system.supports_automatic_rotation
    ensures: RotationConfig.created(
        cipher: cipher,
        target_system: target_system,
        account_identity: account_identity,
        terminate_sessions: terminate_sessions,
        mode: mode,
        rotate_on_access_end: rotate_on_access_end,
        rotation_interval: rotation_interval,
        next_rotation_at: now + rotation_interval,
        enabled: true
    )
    @guidance
        -- Incremental adoption (Goal 5): configurations are created one at a time,
        -- per (cipher, target_system). There is no estate-wide migration step;
        -- registering a target system and creating its first configuration is the
        -- whole onboarding path for that system.
}

-- Scheduling and triggering
--
-- All three rotation triggers — schedule (RotationDue), on demand
-- (TriggerRotationNow) and access events (RotateOnAccessEnd) — converge on the
-- single DispatchRotation chained trigger, so PerformDispatch is the one place
-- that enforces eligibility (enabled, automatic, registration active, no in-flight
-- dispatch). The trigger carries why the rotation fired, recorded on the dispatch.

rule RotationDue {
    when: rotation_config: RotationConfig.next_rotation_at <= now
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: rotation_config.mode = automatic
    requires: not rotation_config.has_in_flight_dispatch
    ensures: DispatchRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: scheduled)
    @guidance
        -- A manual configuration that comes due is NOT dispatched; its due-ness is
        -- the obligation (awaiting_manual_rotation), surfaced for an admin to act
        -- on and clear via RecordManualRotation.
}

rule TriggerRotationNow {
    when: TriggerRotation(_, rotation_config)
    requires: rotation_config.mode = automatic
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: not rotation_config.has_in_flight_dispatch
    ensures: DispatchRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: on_demand)
    @guidance
        -- On-demand rotation (Goal 2): an admin rotates a managed credential
        -- immediately, outside its schedule. Only meaningful for automatic
        -- configurations — a manual one is rotated by hand and recorded via
        -- RecordManualRotation.
}

rule RotateOnAccessEnd {
    when: AccessGrantEnded(cipher)
    let rotation_config = RotationConfig{cipher: cipher}
    requires: exists rotation_config
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: rotation_config.mode = automatic
    requires: rotation_config.rotate_on_access_end
    ensures: DispatchRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: access_end)
    @guidance
        -- Rotation on access events (Goal 2). When an upstream access-control
        -- system signals that a grant over a managed credential has ended — a PAM
        -- lease expiring or being revoked, an AAP grant ending — the cipher's
        -- single rotation configuration (OneConfigPerCipher) is dispatched, if it
        -- is automatic and opted in. The cipher alone resolves the configuration,
        -- so the signal carries no account identity. The signal is intentionally
        -- abstract (AccessEventIntake): rotation depends on the event, not on any
        -- particular leasing spec, so PAM, AAP leasing or a future Secrets Manager
        -- can all emit it. Routing through DispatchRotation means PerformDispatch
        -- re-checks the usual guards, so an in-flight or deregistered config is
        -- safely skipped.
}

rule RedispatchOnReconnect {
    when: _: daemon/ServerConnection.status transitions_to connected
    ensures:
        for rotation_config in RotationConfigs where enabled and mode = automatic and next_rotation_at <= now:
            DispatchRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: reconnect)
    @guidance
        -- On reconnect, re-dispatch every automatic configuration that is due.
        -- Combined with ConnectionDropsLoseDispatches (which marks in-flight
        -- dispatches lost and makes their configs due again), this recovers
        -- rotations interrupted by a connection drop. has_in_flight_dispatch in
        -- PerformDispatch prevents duplicate dispatch for configs already in flight.
}

rule PerformDispatch {
    when: DispatchRotation(rotation_config, source)
    let registration = TargetSystemRegistration{target_system: rotation_config.target_system}
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: rotation_config.mode = automatic
    requires: not rotation_config.has_in_flight_dispatch
    requires: exists registration
    requires: registration.status = active
    ensures:
        let task = daemon/RotationTask.created(
            target_system: rotation_config.target_system,
            cipher: rotation_config.cipher,
            account_identity: rotation_config.account_identity,
            terminate_sessions: rotation_config.terminate_sessions,
            status: pending,
            attempt_count: 0
        )
        TaskDispatch.created(
            rotation_config: rotation_config,
            rotation_task: task,
            status: dispatched,
            source: source,
            dispatched_at: now
        )
        RotationAudited(rotation_config: rotation_config, outcome: dispatched, source: source)
    @guidance
        -- The server is the authoritative creator of the RotationTask. The daemon
        -- observes the new task and executes it (see rotation-daemon.allium /
        -- TaskReceived). See open question on reconciling this with the daemon's
        -- OnTaskDispatched rule.
}

-- Server-side tracking of execution progress (observed from the daemon's
-- RotationTask lifecycle). Each tracking transition also emits the matching audit
-- event so the Phase 1 audit log records every attempt and its outcome (Goal 4).

rule MarkDispatchStarted {
    when: task: daemon/RotationTask.status transitions_to executing
    let dispatch = TaskDispatch{rotation_task: task}
    requires: exists dispatch
    requires: dispatch.status = dispatched
    ensures: dispatch.status = in_progress
}

rule MarkDispatchCompleted {
    when: task: daemon/RotationTask.status transitions_to succeeded
    let dispatch = TaskDispatch{rotation_task: task}
    requires: exists dispatch
    requires: dispatch.status = in_progress
    ensures: dispatch.status = completed
    ensures: dispatch.rotation_config.last_rotation_at = now
    ensures: dispatch.rotation_config.next_rotation_at = now + dispatch.rotation_config.rotation_interval
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: dispatch.rotation_config, outcome: succeeded, source: dispatch.source)
}

rule MarkDispatchFailed {
    when: task: daemon/RotationTask.status transitions_to failed
    let dispatch = TaskDispatch{rotation_task: task}
    requires: exists dispatch
    requires: dispatch.status = in_progress
    ensures: dispatch.status = failed
    ensures: dispatch.rotation_config.next_rotation_at = now + config.failure_retry_delay
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: dispatch.rotation_config, outcome: failed, source: dispatch.source, reason: task.failure_reason)
    @guidance
        -- A failed rotation is rescheduled after a delay. Whether failures should
        -- also raise an alert or escalate is an open question.
}

-- Recovery

rule ConnectionDropsLoseDispatches {
    when: _: daemon/ServerConnection.status transitions_to disconnected
    for dispatch in TaskDispatches where status in {dispatched, in_progress}:
        ensures: DispatchLost(dispatch: dispatch)
    @guidance
        -- A dispatch that was in flight when the connection dropped cannot be
        -- confirmed. Each is marked lost (see MarkDispatchLost) and its
        -- configuration is made due again for re-dispatch on reconnect
        -- (see RedispatchOnReconnect).
}

rule MarkDispatchLost {
    when: DispatchLost(dispatch)
    requires: dispatch.status in {dispatched, in_progress}
    ensures: dispatch.status = lost
    ensures: dispatch.rotation_config.next_rotation_at = now
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: dispatch.rotation_config, outcome: lost, source: dispatch.source)
}

rule InvalidateGrantOnLoss {
    when: dispatch: TaskDispatch.status transitions_to lost
    let grant = CipherWriteGrant{dispatch: dispatch}
    requires: exists grant
    requires: grant.status = issued
    ensures: grant.status = expired
    @guidance
        -- A lost dispatch's grant is invalidated immediately, so a stale write from
        -- the interrupted attempt is rejected even if its TTL has not yet elapsed.
        -- The re-dispatch (RedispatchOnReconnect) issues a fresh grant.
}

-- Cipher write authorisation (the nonce)
--
-- The daemon writes the rotated, encrypted password back to a cipher by presenting
-- the grant issued with its dispatch (CipherWriteIntake). The server verifies the
-- grant is issued, unexpired and bound to the submitted cipher, then persists the
-- ciphertext and consumes the grant. An invalid grant is refused and audited.

rule IssueCipherWriteGrant {
    when: dispatch: TaskDispatch.created
    requires: dispatch.status = dispatched
    ensures: CipherWriteGrant.created(
        dispatch: dispatch,
        cipher: dispatch.rotation_config.cipher,
        created_at: now,
        expires_at: now + config.cipher_write_grant_ttl,
        status: issued
    )
    @guidance
        -- Each dispatch carries exactly one cipher-write grant, minted when the
        -- dispatch is created and delivered to the daemon with the task.
}

rule CipherWriteGrantExpires {
    when: grant: CipherWriteGrant.expires_at <= now
    requires: grant.status = issued
    ensures: grant.status = expired
}

rule AcceptCipherUpdate {
    when: SubmitCipherUpdate(grant, cipher, new_password)
    requires: grant.status = issued
    requires: grant.expires_at > now
    requires: grant.cipher = cipher
    ensures: cipher.password = new_password
    ensures: grant.status = consumed
    @guidance
        -- new_password is ciphertext the daemon encrypted with the organization key;
        -- the server persists it without decrypting (zero-knowledge). Consuming the
        -- grant makes the write single-use.
}

rule RejectCipherUpdate {
    when: SubmitCipherUpdate(grant, cipher, new_password)
    requires: grant.status != issued or grant.expires_at <= now or grant.cipher != cipher
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: grant.dispatch.rotation_config, outcome: write_rejected)
    @guidance
        -- The write is refused: the grant is expired, already consumed, or does not
        -- bind to the submitted cipher. The daemon surfaces this as a failed attempt
        -- (rotation-daemon.allium / RotationAttemptFailed), which retries the whole
        -- rotation under a fresh grant.
}

-- Manual rotation fallback (Goal 3)

rule RecordManualRotation {
    when: RecordManualRotation(actor, rotation_config)
    requires: rotation_config.mode = manual
    ensures: rotation_config.last_rotation_at = now
    ensures: rotation_config.next_rotation_at = now + rotation_config.rotation_interval
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: rotation_config, outcome: manual_recorded, actor: actor)
    @guidance
        -- For a manually-managed configuration the daemon never rotates; instead
        -- the obligation surfaces (awaiting_manual_rotation) and an admin records
        -- that they rotated the credential out of band. Recording resets the
        -- schedule and writes the audit trail, exactly as a successful automatic
        -- rotation would — an honest record that the obligation was met without
        -- pretending the system can self-rotate.
}

-- Audit
--
-- RotationAudited is emitted on every rotation attempt and outcome — a dispatch
-- (the attempt), its success, failure or loss, a refused cipher write
-- (write_rejected), and a recorded manual rotation (Goal 4). It is a pure sink: no
-- rule chains from it. Durable, append-only,
-- tamper-evident (WORM) storage is the existing Phase 1 audit log's job and is out
-- of scope here; this spec models the event, not its persistence (mirroring
-- pam.allium's LeaseActivationAudited). `source` carries why the rotation fired
-- (scheduled / on_demand / access_end / reconnect) on every dispatch-derived
-- outcome; `reason` is the daemon's failure_reason on a failed outcome; `actor` is
-- the recording admin on a manual_recorded outcome (whose channel is implicit in
-- the outcome). Each is absent where it does not apply.

-- Invariants

invariant OneRegistrationPerTargetSystem {
    for a in TargetSystemRegistrations:
        for b in TargetSystemRegistrations:
            a != b implies a.target_system != b.target_system
}

invariant OneConfigPerCipher {
    -- A cipher is under at most one rotation configuration. The configuration names
    -- the target system, so the cipher uniquely identifies what to rotate and where
    -- (relied on by RotateOnAccessEnd and the RotationConfig{cipher} lookup).
    for a in RotationConfigs:
        for b in RotationConfigs:
            a != b implies a.cipher != b.cipher
}

invariant AtMostOneInFlightDispatchPerConfig {
    for rotation_config in RotationConfigs:
        rotation_config.in_flight_dispatches.count <= 1
}

invariant AutomaticConfigTargetsCapableSystem {
    -- An automatic configuration only ever sits on a system the daemon can rotate;
    -- everything else must be managed manually.
    for c in RotationConfigs where mode = automatic:
        c.target_system.supports_automatic_rotation
}

invariant ManualConfigNeverDispatched {
    -- A manual configuration is never sent to the daemon; its rotations are
    -- recorded out of band. (Mode is fixed at creation in Phase 1 — see the
    -- mode-switching open question.)
    for c in RotationConfigs where mode = manual:
        c.dispatches.count = 0
}

-- Surfaces

surface RotationAdministration {
    facing _: Administrator

    provides:
        RegisterDaemon(name, client_id)
        RegisterTargetSystem(target_system)
        CreateRotationConfig(cipher, target_system, account_identity, terminate_sessions, rotation_interval, mode, rotate_on_access_end)
}

surface DaemonRegistrationView {
    facing _: Administrator
    context registration: DaemonRegistration

    exposes:
        registration.name
        registration.client_id
        registration.status

    provides:
        RevokeDaemon(registration)
            when registration.status = enrolled
}

surface TargetSystemRegistrationView {
    facing _: Administrator
    context registration: TargetSystemRegistration

    exposes:
        registration.target_system.name
        registration.target_system.supports_automatic_rotation
        registration.status

    provides:
        DisableTargetSystem(registration)
            when registration.status = active
        EnableTargetSystem(registration)
            when registration.status = disabled
}

surface RotationConfigView {
    -- A single rotation configuration: its schedule, mode, the standing
    -- obligation for a manual config, and the actions available to an admin.
    facing admin: Administrator
    context rotation_config: RotationConfig

    exposes:
        rotation_config.cipher
        rotation_config.target_system.name
        rotation_config.account_identity
        rotation_config.mode
        rotation_config.enabled
        rotation_config.rotate_on_access_end
        rotation_config.rotation_interval
        rotation_config.last_rotation_at
        rotation_config.next_rotation_at
        rotation_config.has_in_flight_dispatch
        -- The flag that drives the manual-obligation prompt (Goal 3).
        rotation_config.awaiting_manual_rotation

    provides:
        -- On-demand rotation for an automatic configuration (Goal 2).
        TriggerRotation(admin, rotation_config)
            when rotation_config.mode = automatic
             and rotation_config.enabled
             and not rotation_config.has_in_flight_dispatch
        -- Record an out-of-band rotation for a manual configuration (Goal 3).
        RecordManualRotation(admin, rotation_config)
            when rotation_config.mode = manual
}

surface AccessEventIntake {
    -- Inbound integration boundary: an upstream access-control system reports that
    -- a grant over a managed credential has ended. Abstract by design — PAM, AAP
    -- leasing or (later) Secrets Manager may all be the source.
    facing _: AccessEventSource
    context cipher: daemon/Cipher

    provides:
        AccessGrantEnded(cipher)
}

surface CipherWriteIntake {
    -- The daemon writes a rotated, encrypted password back to a cipher, presenting
    -- the grant (nonce) the server issued with its dispatch. This is the only cipher
    -- operation the daemon's scoped credential permits.
    facing _: RotationDaemon
    context grant: CipherWriteGrant

    provides:
        SubmitCipherUpdate(grant, cipher, new_password)

    @guarantee NonceBoundWrite
        -- A cipher write is accepted only against an issued, unexpired grant whose
        -- cipher matches the submission, and the grant is consumed on use (single
        -- write). new_password is ciphertext encrypted with the organization key; the
        -- server stores it without decrypting (zero-knowledge).
}

-- Open questions

-- Resolved: a daemon is registered server-side (DaemonRegistration) and authenticates
-- with a provisioned access token via an OAuth2 client-credentials grant; the session
-- lifecycle (refresh, revoke) is modelled in rotation-daemon.allium. The vault key
-- grant the daemon needs to write ciphers remains an open question there.
open question "Multi-daemon routing: when more than one daemon is connected, how does the server choose which daemon receives a dispatch, and is recovery scoped per-daemon rather than to all in-flight dispatches?"
open question "Reconcile task creation: both the daemon's OnTaskDispatched and the server's PerformDispatch model RotationTask creation. Confirm the server is authoritative and adjust the daemon's dispatch entry point accordingly."
open question "Failure handling beyond rescheduling: should repeated rotation failures raise an alert, escalate, or disable the configuration after a threshold?"
open question "Should disabling a target system or a configuration cancel in-flight dispatches, or only stop new ones?"
open question "Manual obligation escalation: beyond surfacing awaiting_manual_rotation, should a manual configuration whose obligation stays unmet past a threshold escalate — notify, flag severity, or raise an alert?"
open question "Access-event emitter binding: which upstream systems wire into AccessGrantEnded (PAM leases, AAP grants, ...)? The signal-shape question is resolved — it carries only the cipher, which uniquely resolves the configuration to rotate (OneConfigPerCipher), so no account_identity is needed — but the concrete set of emitters and how they register against AccessEventIntake is still open."
open question "On-demand and access-triggered dispatches record a source on the TaskDispatch but not the triggering admin (only manual_recorded carries an actor). Should the audit attribute the on-demand actor end to end?"
open question "Mode switching: can an admin move a configuration between automatic and manual after creation, and if so what becomes of its schedule and any in-flight dispatch? (ManualConfigNeverDispatched assumes mode is fixed at creation.)"
open question "Secrets Manager support (Phase 1 non-goal): a future configuration would point at an SM secret rather than a daemon/Cipher. Confirm the dispatch, recovery, scheduling and audit machinery here carries an SM rotation task unchanged, and decide whether to generalise the cipher seam or add a parallel SM configuration."
